course: history
lesson: 28
title: Explorers: Cook and the Pacific
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Long before any European ship arrived, Polynesian navigators crossed the Pacific in double canoes, reading the stars, the waves and the flight of birds, and settled islands from Hawaii to New Zealand.
explain: In 1642 the Dutch sailor Abel Tasman reached Tasmania and New Zealand, and in the 1760s and 1770s the British captain James Cook made three great voyages and mapped coasts so well that some of his charts were used for centuries.
explain: Cook kept his crew healthy with fresh food and sauerkraut, carried scientists and artists who drew strange new animals like the kangaroo, and sailed further south than anyone, until walls of ice turned him back.
explain: Think of a new neighbour moving in and telling everyone he found the street: when Cook claimed the east coast of Australia for Britain in 1770, Aboriginal people had lived there for more than 50,000 years.
explain: So every explorer's story has two sides: great courage and skill, and the colonies that later took land from the people already there.
fact: Polynesian navigators crossed the Pacific in double canoes, finding their way by the stars, waves and birds, and settled islands from Hawaii to New Zealand.
fact: In 1642 the Dutch sailor Abel Tasman reached Tasmania and New Zealand; Tasmania is named after him, and New Zealand after the Dutch region of Zeeland.
fact: James Cook, a British naval captain, made three great voyages in the 1760s and 1770s, mapping New Zealand, the east coast of Australia and many Pacific islands.
fact: Cook fought scurvy with fresh food and sauerkraut and lost far fewer men to it than earlier voyages.
fact: Cook carried scientists and artists, such as the botanist Joseph Banks, who collected thousands of new plants and drew animals like the kangaroo.
fact: Tupaia, a navigator from Tahiti, sailed with Cook, helped guide the ship and helped him talk with the Māori.
fact: In 1770 Cook landed at Botany Bay and claimed the east coast of Australia for Britain, though Aboriginal people had lived there for over 50,000 years.
fact: The Māori had arrived in New Zealand from Polynesia about 700 years ago; some of their meetings with Cook were friendly and some were violent.
fact: Cook was killed in Hawaii in 1779 in a fight that started over a stolen boat.
fact: Cook sailed further south than anyone before, crossing the Antarctic Circle, but turned back at the ice, and Antarctica was first seen in 1820.
fact: Explorers were brave and skilled, but their voyages also opened the way for colonies that took land from local peoples.
exercise: How did Polynesians find islands without maps? | By reading the stars, waves and birds | By following a compass made of gold and silver | By using satellites | By asking passing ships
exercise: What kind of boats did Polynesian navigators use? | Double canoes | Steamships | Viking longships | Rowing boats made of iron
exercise: Which island is named after Abel Tasman? | Tasmania | Hawaii in the north | Tahiti in the south | Sri Lanka near India
exercise: Where does the name New Zealand come from? | The Dutch region of Zeeland | A Māori word for the deep blue sea | Captain Cook's wife | A Spanish king
exercise: Which country did James Cook sail for? | Britain | The Netherlands | Spain and its king | Portugal
exercise: What did Cook map? | New Zealand and the east coast of Australia | The Strait of Magellan and the coast of Chile | The coast of Norway | The river Nile
exercise: How did Cook fight scurvy? | With fresh food and sauerkraut | With more salt meat and less water | By banning sleep | With sea water
exercise: Which animal amazed the scientists in Australia? | The kangaroo | The African elephant | The wild horse | The camel of the desert
exercise: Who was Joseph Banks? | A botanist who sailed with Cook | A Dutch captain who mapped Tasmania | A Hawaiian king | A Māori chief
exercise: Who was Tupaia? | A Tahitian navigator who sailed with Cook | A British admiral who never left London | The first king of Australia | A Spanish conquistador
exercise: Who already lived at Botany Bay when Cook arrived? | Aboriginal people | Nobody at all | Dutch farmers from Java | The Māori from New Zealand
exercise: Where had the Māori come from? | Polynesia | Australia's coasts | Japan and Korea | South America
exercise: How did Cook die? | In a fight in Hawaii over a stolen boat | Of old age in London, in his own bed | In a shipwreck in the ice | In a battle in France
exercise: Why did Cook turn back in the far south? | Walls of ice blocked the way | He ran out of maps | His crew wanted to go home for Christmas | The sea became too warm
exercise: When was Antarctica first seen? | In 1820 | In 1066, by Vikings | In 1492, by Columbus | In 1969, by plane
exercise: What are the two sides of an explorer's story? | Courage and skill, and colonies that took land | Fame and money only, and nothing else at all | Good weather and bad weather | Maps and pictures
test: Lucy says Cook discovered New Zealand. What would a Māori historian say? | Our ancestors arrived about 700 years ago | Nobody lived there before Cook came in 1769 | Thank you for finding it | Tasman was the first person there
test: Why were Cook's crews healthier than Magellan's? | They ate fresh food and sauerkraut against scurvy | They had modern medicine and doctors on board | They sailed only in summer | They were all younger
test: If Polynesians had no maps or compasses, what did their great voyages show? | They were some of the greatest sailors in history | They got lucky every time and never once got lost at sea | Someone else steered for them | They only sailed a few metres
test: Why did Cook carry artists as well as sailors? | To draw the plants, animals and places they found | To paint the ship each week | To make the captain's portrait every single day of the voyage | Cameras were too heavy
test: A cartoon shows Cook planting a flag on an empty beach in Australia. What is missing? | The Aboriginal people who had lived there for over 50,000 years | Kangaroos wearing hats | Cook's dog | Nothing is missing, it shows the land exactly as it was before
test: why'd cook even need a tahitian guy on the ship | Tupaia knew the Pacific and helped talk with the Māori | He was the ship's cook and made very good meals | He was a stowaway | He did not need anyone
test: What would Cook's crew see if they kept sailing south past the ice? | The land of Antarctica | A warm tropical island | The North Pole | Australia again
test: Why did Cook's maps bring more Europeans to New Zealand? | Good maps made the voyage easier to plan | The maps were sold as treasure maps | They showed gold on every hill | The Māori sent the maps to Europe
test: How could a small quarrel over a boat turn deadly in Hawaii? | Misunderstandings between peoples could turn violent | Boats were sacred weapons that only kings could touch | Cook ordered a war over it | It never happened
test: In a game your explorer gets a bonus for 'finding' Tasmania in 1642. Who should get the real credit for knowing it first? | The Aboriginal people of Tasmania | Abel Tasman alone, with his crew of Dutch sailors | Captain Cook | Nobody, it was empty
exam: Identify the method by which Polynesian navigators located islands across the Pacific Ocean. | Observation of stars, swells and birds | The use of magnetic compasses supplied from Europe | Reliance upon printed charts | Communication by radio
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the condition of the east coast of Australia at the time of Cook's landing in seventeen hundred and seventy? | It had been inhabited by Aboriginal peoples for over fifty thousand years | It was uninhabited | It was a Dutch colony | It was governed by the Māori
exam: Ascertain the means by which James Cook diminished the incidence of scurvy among his crew. | The provision of fresh food and sauerkraut | The prohibition of all sleep at sea | The consumption of sea water | The reduction of the crew to ten men
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the death of James Cook. | He was killed in Hawaii in seventeen hundred and seventy-nine | He died in Antarctica | He perished at Botany Bay | He died peacefully in London in the company of his family and friends
